"Groups numeric values by leading digits while retaining trailing digits as countable leaves.\n\nUse when: A small dataset needs a distribution view that preserves approximate raw values. Readers can use a clear place-value key to recover observations.\n\nAvoid when: Values span many orders of magnitude or produce very long leaf rows.\n\nVisual grammar: The stem encodes leading digits or a rounded numeric interval. Each leaf encodes the trailing digit of one included observation.\n\nBuild: Choose numeric precision and split each observation into stem and leaf. Sort stems numerically and leaves within each row. Align a monospace text display and add a key such as 12|3 = 123.\n\nCheck: Check leaf count equals the included sample size. Verify negative values and decimal rounding follow a documented rule. Confirm the key reconstructs representative observations correctly.\n\nPitfalls: Changing precision can hide important differences between values. Use a histogram when dense stems require tiny text or numerous split rows.\n\nExample: Display synthetic test scores 42–98 using tens as stems and units as sorted leaves.\n\nAlternatives: Histogram, Wilkinson dot plot, Strip plot."
